    <h1>Prenatal Vitamins: What You Need to Know</h1>

    <p>A prenatal vitamin helps fill nutritional gaps during pregnancy. While a balanced diet is the best source of nutrients, a prenatal vitamin provides extra support for the nutrients that are most critical during pregnancy. This guide cites NHS advice for England and ACOG advice for the United States, and shows both where they differ.</p>

    <h2>Key Nutrients in a Prenatal Vitamin</h2>

    <h3>Folic Acid (600 mcg)</h3>
    <p>Folic acid is the synthetic form of folate, a B vitamin that reduces the risk of neural tube defects (NTDs) such as spina bifida. The neural tube forms and closes in the first few weeks of pregnancy, often before many women know they are pregnant. This is why ACOG recommends 600 micrograms of folic acid a day during pregnancy, including a daily prenatal vitamin containing 400 micrograms.</p>

    <h3>Iron (27 mg)</h3>
    <p>Iron supports the increased blood volume during pregnancy and helps prevent anemia. Your blood volume increases by about 50% during pregnancy, and your baby is also building their own iron stores.</p>

    <h3>Calcium (1,000 mg)</h3>
    <p>Calcium supports the development of the baby's bones and teeth. If you do not get enough calcium in your diet, your body will take calcium from your bones to support the baby.</p>

    <h3>Vitamin D (600 IU)</h3>
    <p>Vitamin D supports calcium absorption and bone health. It also plays a role in immune function. Many women are deficient in vitamin D, especially those who live in northern climates or have darker skin.</p>

    <h3>DHA (200-300 mg)</h3>
    <p>DHA (docosahexaenoic acid) is an omega-3 fatty acid that supports the baby's brain and eye development. It is found in fatty fish and algae-based supplements.</p>

    <h3>Iodine (220 mcg)</h3>
    <p>Iodine is essential for the baby's thyroid function and brain development. Mild iodine deficiency is common in the United States.</p>

    <h3>Choline (450 mg)</h3>
    <p>Choline supports the baby's brain development and helps prevent neural tube defects. Most prenatal vitamins do not contain enough choline, so dietary sources (eggs, meat, fish) are important.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy); NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/).</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />
    <h2>Folic acid dose and neural tube defects</h2>

    <p>The NHS recommends 400 micrograms of folic acid a day, ideally starting 3 months before you try to get pregnant, and continuing for the first 12 weeks. This reduces the chance of a neural tube defect such as spina bifida.</p>

    <p>ACOG lists 600 micrograms a day during pregnancy, including a daily prenatal vitamin containing 400 micrograms. In the UK, a previous affected pregnancy, diabetes, or epilepsy medicine means a prescribed 5mg dose.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/); ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy).</em></p>

    <h2>Iron and anaemia screening in pregnancy</h2>

    <p>Iron makes red blood cells, which carry oxygen around your body. Without enough iron you may feel more tired than usual and you can have anaemia. Your GP or midwife will recommend iron supplements if you need them.</p>

    <p>The NHS checks your iron at your booking appointment and again at around 28 weeks. ACOG lists 27 milligrams of iron a day in pregnancy. If iron upsets your stomach, contact your clinician.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/); NHS, Iron (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron); ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy).</em></p>

    <h2>DHA and omega-3 in pregnancy</h2>

    <p>ACOG lists omega-3 fatty acids among the nutrients needed in pregnancy. The NHS sets no DHA target and advises no more than 2 portions of oily fish a week, and to avoid swordfish, marlin and shark.</p>

    <p>Oily fish can contain pollutants such as dioxins, and tuna has more mercury than other fish. If you do not eat fish, talk to your midwife or GP about an algae-based omega-3 supplement.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy); NHS, Foods to avoid in pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/foods-to-avoid/).</em></p>

    <h2>Calcium and vitamin D</h2>

    <p>The NHS says adults need 700mg of calcium a day. ACOG gives a higher pregnancy figure: 1,000 milligrams a day for ages 19 to 50, and 1,300 milligrams for ages 14 to 18.</p>

    <p>The NHS recommends 10 micrograms of vitamin D a day from early October to late March, the same as 400 IU. ACOG lists 600 IU, and no more than 100 micrograms a day. If you think you might not get enough, contact your clinician.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Calcium (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/calcium/); NHS, Vitamin D (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d/); NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/); ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy).</em></p>

    <h2>Iodine and choline</h2>

    <p>The NHS says adults need 140 micrograms of iodine a day, from dairy, eggs, sea fish and shellfish. ACOG lists 220 micrograms a day in pregnancy, which is higher.</p>

    <p>ACOG lists choline at 450 milligrams a day, and most prenatal vitamins do not contain enough. Do not take high doses of iodine for long periods.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Iodine (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iodine/); ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy).</em></p>

    <h2>How to read a prenatal supplement label</h2>

    <p>Microgram is sometimes written as mcg or μg, and vitamin D is sometimes measured in IU, where 10 micrograms is 400 IU. Check the label for retinol.</p>

    <p>If you need higher dose folic acid or other supplements, speak to a GP. You may be able to get free vitamin supplements with Healthy Start.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/); NHS, Vitamin A (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-a/).</em></p>

    <h2>Vitamin A and retinol caution</h2>

    <p>The NHS says you should not take cod liver oil or supplements containing vitamin A when you are pregnant, and should avoid liver. Too much vitamin A can harm your baby's development.</p>

    <p>ACOG lists vitamin A at 750 micrograms a day for ages 14 to 18 and 770 micrograms for ages 19 to 50. If you are unsure whether a supplement is safe, contact your clinician.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Vitamin A (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-a/); NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/); ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy).</em></p>

    <h2>When to Start Taking Prenatal Vitamins</h2>

    <p>ACOG recommends starting a prenatal vitamin at least one month before conception. Because many pregnancies are unplanned, it is best for all women who could become pregnant to take a prenatal vitamin daily.</p>

    <p>If you are already pregnant, start taking a prenatal vitamin as soon as you find out. The NHS recommends starting folic acid ideally 3 months before you try to get pregnant, or as soon as you find out.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Pregnancy vitamins and supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/pregnancy-vitamins-and-supplements/); ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy).</em></p>

