    <h2>Choosing a Prenatal Vitamin</h2>

    <p>Not all prenatal vitamins are created equal. Here is what to look for:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Folic acid:</strong> 400 mcg in a prenatal vitamin (600 mcg total daily)</li>
      <li><strong>Iron:</strong> 27 mg</li>
      <li><strong>Calcium:</strong> 200-300 mg (most women need additional calcium from diet or a separate supplement)</li>
      <li><strong>Vitamin D:</strong> 600 IU</li>
      <li><strong>DHA:</strong> 200-300 mg</li>
      <li><strong>Iodine:</strong> 220 mcg</li>
      <li><strong>Choline:</strong> 450 mg (often not included in prenatal vitamins)</li>
      <li><strong>Vitamin A:</strong> avoid supplements containing retinol, and avoid liver</li>
    </ul>

<p>Always discuss supplements with your clinician before starting them. Some supplements may interact with medications or may not be appropriate for your specific situation.</p>

    <h2>Foods to Focus On</h2>

    <p>A prenatal vitamin supplements a healthy diet, and it does not replace one. The NHS advises a varied diet, with no need to eat for two. Focus on:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Leafy greens:</strong> Folate, calcium, iron</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> Choline, protein, vitamin D</li>
      <li><strong>Fatty fish:</strong> DHA, vitamin D (limit to 2 portions per week)</li>
      <li><strong>Lean meat:</strong> Iron, protein, choline</li>
      <li><strong>Dairy:</strong> Calcium, vitamin D, protein</li>
      <li><strong>Whole grains:</strong> Iron, B vitamins, fiber</li>
      <li><strong>Legumes:</strong> Folate, iron, protein, fiber</li>
      <li><strong>Fortified cereals and bread:</strong> Iron, folic acid, iodine</li>
      <li><strong>Carrots and sweet potatoes:</strong> Beta-carotene, which the body can convert to vitamin A</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, Have a healthy diet in pregnancy (https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/have-a-healthy-diet/); ACOG, Healthy Eating During Pregnancy (https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy).</em></p>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You cannot tolerate your prenatal vitamin (nausea, constipation)</li>
      <li>You have questions about which supplements to take</li>
      <li>You have a medical condition that affects your nutritional needs</li>
      <li>You have had a previous pregnancy affected by a neural tube defect</li>
      <li>You follow a strict vegan diet and want advice on iodine or omega-3</li>
      <li>You are taking medicine for epilepsy or HIV and need a higher folic acid dose</li>
    </ul>

